update public.questions set explanation = $e$The conjugate of a + bi is a − bi: keep the real part and flip the sign of the imaginary part. Here 4 − 7i becomes 4 + 7i. Check: (4 − 7i)(4 + 7i) = 16 + 49 = 65, which is real, as every conjugate product is.$e$ where id = '00774af6-df06-4d92-a9f8-e11be88d428d';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$In the fetch cycle the instruction is read from memory into the MDR (memory data register) and then copied into the CIR (current instruction register), where it is decoded. The MAR holds only an address, so it is not on the path that carries the instruction itself.$e$ where id = '0102773b-99fb-4ef6-baec-c69661580662';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$A SELECT reads rows and never changes them. The WHERE clause only filters which rows are returned, so the table is left as it was. Any change to the data needs INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE.$e$ where id = '011ded75-da45-45bc-9a44-5ec91f7ea157';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Electrical power is P = VI. Substituting V = 240 V and I = 2 A gives P = 240 × 2 = 480 W. Check the units: volts × amperes = watts.$e$ where id = '01303350-728f-4712-9c7a-e634221677f1';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$The forces are perpendicular, so the resultant comes from Pythagoras: R = √(3² + 4²) = √(9 + 16) = √25 = 5 N. Adding 3 N and 4 N directly (7 N) ignores the directions and is wrong for perpendicular forces.$e$ where id = '018cc419-e579-40c1-b518-9625bc51d238';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Work from the inside out. g(x) = 2x, so f(g(x)) = f(2x). Applying f adds 5 to 2x, giving 2x + 5. The other order, g(f(x)) = 2x + 10, is a different function.$e$ where id = '036b6917-9a9b-45f6-a036-5bb8e6cedc9b';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$For X ~ B(n, p) the mean is E(X) = np. Here n = 10 and p = 0.4, so E(X) = 10 × 0.4 = 4. The variance np(1 − p) = 2.4 is a different quantity and is not asked for.$e$ where id = '038855da-ea70-4f1b-99a5-ab9188693aeb';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$A stationary point is where dy/dx = 0. Differentiating gives dy/dx = 2x − 6, so 2x − 6 = 0 and x = 3. The coefficient of x² is positive, so this point is a minimum (y = 9 − 18 + 4 = −5 there).$e$ where id = '03ca9c50-b39f-4d80-999f-4c6dd57ef41a';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Write a sin x + b cos x as R sin(x + α). Expanding gives R cos α = a and R sin α = b, so R = √(a² + b²) = √(3² + 4²) = √25 = 5. The angle α (tan α = 4/3) is not asked for here.$e$ where id = '042e4f06-e9ab-4493-8346-617fd244e56f';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Pressure is force per unit area: p = F/A = 600 N ÷ 2 m² = 300 Pa. The unit N/m² is the pascal.$e$ where id = '049dad1a-fa89-4664-8e84-745f3a35edd3';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$The Poisson distribution approximates the binomial when n is large and p is small, so that np stays moderate and the model becomes Po(np). Rare events among many trials is exactly that setting. A large p gives a poor match, because the trials are then far from rare.$e$ where id = '050fe135-38be-459f-a392-31cf20ab39cb';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$An antiderivative of sin x is −cos x. Evaluating from 0 to π/2: [−cos(π/2)] − [−cos 0] = 0 − (−1) = 1. The lower limit is subtracted, which is where the sign flips.$e$ where id = '06c557f1-903a-4fa8-978c-4a765ae2fea6';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Density is mass per unit volume: ρ = m/V = 4000 kg ÷ 0.5 m³ = 8000 kg/m³. Check the units: kg divided by m³ gives kg/m³.$e$ where id = '0705ae6c-4993-4af9-b8ea-78789328b1aa';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$In the von Neumann model the program counter holds the address of the next instruction to be fetched, and it is incremented after each fetch so that it moves through the program in order. The data values themselves are held in memory and the registers, not in the PC.$e$ where id = '072ae34e-3fad-4078-a8b1-3c3f72abc32d';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$σ is the square root of the variance: √25 = 5. Standardise with z = (x − μ)/σ = (60 − 50)/5 = 2. Using 25 as the standard deviation would give z = 0.4, which is the common slip.$e$ where id = '07c5404b-cf27-481c-bce7-c56e99845bee';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$The expected value of a binomial distribution is np. With 20 independent trials and p = 0.7, E(X) = 20 × 0.7 = 14. Independence of the shots is what makes the binomial model valid here.$e$ where id = '088a09fe-74ae-4da3-8c2c-01f90620fd37';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$The area under a force–extension graph is the work done in stretching the material, because work = force × extension summed over the stretch. The energy is then stored in the material. For a non-linear graph the area is found by counting squares or integrating.$e$ where id = '09635e15-0a12-4106-a4f0-24c06735389b';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$P(X < 0.5) = ∫₀^0.5 2x dx = [x²] from 0 to 0.5 = 0.25. As a check, the density integrates to 1 over 0 ≤ x ≤ 1, so it is a valid density.$e$ where id = '09f0139e-3007-4fe6-b507-b020bc004308';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Write 1 + i = √2 cis(π/4). Then (1 + i)⁸ = (√2)⁸ cis(8 × π/4) = 16 cis 2π = 16. Raising to a power raises the modulus to that power and multiplies the argument by it.$e$ where id = '0a5369c3-9393-40a7-a933-bdfb49f198ab';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$The modulus of a vector is its magnitude. For 3i + 4j it is √(3² + 4²) = √25 = 5, found by Pythagoras on the components.$e$ where id = '0a6ee2f7-a13c-43a1-82e5-5c818a5e05d6';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity: a = Δv/t = (30 − 0)/6 = 5 m s⁻². The units are metres per second per second.$e$ where id = '0b8460f2-848c-4c62-b85d-9c520bd1674a';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$cos 60° = ½, and 60° is the standard angle to recall for this value. In the range 0° to 90° the solution is unique, so θ = 60°.$e$ where id = '0c0ecd42-a9bf-46de-9e69-c9a95569888a';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Density ρ = m ÷ V = 200 g ÷ 50 cm³ = 4 g/cm³. The result is in grams per cubic centimetre, which is the unit the question asks for.$e$ where id = '0c46bb6d-11d8-439b-b4fc-2c6cc02fa7fe';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$By the binomial theorem, the x² term of (1 + x)⁵ has coefficient C(5,2) = 5!/(2!·3!) = 10. Check by counting: there are 10 ways to choose which 2 of the 5 factors contribute an x.$e$ where id = '0c83de6c-ebff-4846-9175-4769bcbb3ef2';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$For grouped data each class is represented by its midpoint. The estimated mean is Σ(f × midpoint) ÷ Σf. It is an estimate, because the individual values inside each class are not known.$e$ where id = '0cd8b83f-b3fb-41e0-b4d2-24cd05d251f4';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$E(X) = ∫₀² x · (x/2) dx = ∫₀² x²/2 dx = [x³/6] from 0 to 2 = 8/6 = 4/3. Check that the density integrates to 1: ∫₀² x/2 dx = 1.$e$ where id = '0eb92e4a-d11e-404e-be39-93707a06bfa7';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$The charge on a capacitor is Q = CV. Here C = 10 μF = 10 × 10⁻⁶ F and V = 12 V, so Q = 120 μC.$e$ where id = '0ed5bc0a-074c-498d-b128-a17de096a7b2';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Momentum is the product of mass and velocity: p = mv. It is a vector quantity pointing in the direction of the velocity, measured in kg m s⁻¹.$e$ where id = '0f1e656d-b35d-439b-a18f-6eab9ec0a909';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$1 km = 1000 m and 1 h = 3600 s, so 72 km/h = 72 000 m ÷ 3600 s = 20 m/s. Dividing by 3.6 gives the same answer.$e$ where id = '101972a2-9328-4585-9a58-163a51912341';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$The sum to infinity of a geometric progression with |r| < 1 is S∞ = a/(1 − r). Here a = 6 and r = ½, so S∞ = 6/(½) = 12. The series converges because |r| < 1.$e$ where id = '107c4109-adf7-4b5d-8476-25e53280a2c1';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Conditional probability is P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B)/P(B) = 0.15/0.4 = 0.375. The unconditional P(A) = 0.5 is not the answer, because the question restricts attention to outcomes where B has occurred.$e$ where id = '10dd90aa-c777-403c-aafd-d6eef6ef0b90';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$A moment is the turning effect of a force about a point. It equals the force multiplied by the perpendicular distance from the point to the line of action of the force. Only the perpendicular component contributes.$e$ where id = '1118b51e-29ad-4084-b3ab-aa1d93b39afa';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Let u = x², so du = 2x dx. Then ∫ 2x e^(x²) dx = ∫ e^u du = e^u + c = e^(x²) + c. Differentiating e^(x²) gives 2x e^(x²), which confirms the result.$e$ where id = '1136d116-3303-4b18-9618-8d119cda86f8';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Hooke's law: within the limit of proportionality the extension is proportional to the load, so F = kx, where k is the stiffness (spring constant). A graph of F against x is a straight line through the origin.$e$ where id = '114dd5cf-88de-4dab-9813-96bdaed3cafc';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$1011 in binary is 1×8 + 0×4 + 1×2 + 1×1 = 8 + 2 + 1 = 11 in denary. The place values from the left are 8, 4, 2, 1.$e$ where id = '124bf971-b886-4312-90dc-0981e325345e';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$For a Poisson distribution the mean and the variance are both equal to λ, so Var(X) = 2.5. Do not confuse the variance with the standard deviation, which would be √2.5.$e$ where id = '139d9dc5-0c64-4c1d-a842-c5f506d26f7d';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$tan 45° = 1, because sin 45° = cos 45° = 1/√2. In the range 0° to 90° the angle is unique, so θ = 45°.$e$ where id = '153e75d7-8232-45e6-ade4-7da681256fda';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$The wave equation is v = fλ. Here f = 2 Hz and λ = 3 m, so v = 2 × 3 = 6 m/s. Check the units: Hz × m = m/s.$e$ where id = '16588ca5-15d6-4db1-b876-10671dbf0486';
update public.questions set explanation = $e$Variance = Σx²/n − x̄², with x̄ = Σx/n = 60/5 = 12. So the variance is 1000/5 − 12² = 200 − 144 = 40. Σx²/n is the mean of the squares, not the square of the mean, and the two must not be confused.$e$ where id = '167c4a25-f072-4a18-a90b-a197bfd392d2';
